  I'll flag the decomposition properly once I know what shape the system takes.

  Next fork, and it's the one that sets the size of everything downstream: [1mwhat counts as a "change," and who causes it.
[0m
  - [1mSomeone else changed a shared task[0m — this is a collaboration change feed. It needs real accounts, shared tasks with
    permissions, a record of who did what and when, and semantics for "care about" (subscribed? assigned? created it?
    commented?). The notification system is genuinely the interesting part, and the tasks layer underneath it gets
    substantially bigger — sharing and identity are not cheap to retrofit.
  - [1mThe task changed on its own[0m — due today, now overdue, reminder fires. This works with a single user and needs no
    accounts at all. It's a scheduler reading dates, not a change feed. Dramatically smaller, and the whole "who else
    can see this" question disappears.
  - [1mBoth[0m — two mechanisms that happen to share a delivery path and an inbox UI. Worth doing deliberately, not by
    accident.

  The thing that's expensive to change later is identity and sharing. If tasks are ever shared, that belongs in the data
  model from the first line; bolting multi-user onto a single-user store is a rewrite, not a migration. Due-date
  reminders, by contrast, can be added to a single-user app cheaply at almost any point.

  My recommendation: [1mcollaboration change feed[0m, because "tasks they care about" is phrasing that only makes sense when
  someone [3melse[0m can change your tasks — but I'd rather hear it from you than build a sharing model on my reading of one
  sentence.
